# Example profile for: banned -p planner
# Install as ~/.config/banned/profiles/planner.rules (owned by the account that
# runs the check, NOT inside the project tree, not writable by the agent's user):
#
#   install -m 644 planner.rules.example ~/.config/banned/profiles/planner.rules
#
# Same record format as BANNED_RULES in banned.sh:  name::regex::reason
# Blank lines and '#' in column 0 are skipped. These ADD to the global rules.
# A profile with no active rules is an error (exit 2), never "clean".
#
# Network use is banned for the planner by its CLAUDE.md. As with the global
# rules, mentions in comments and lookalikes will also match; that is intended.
# Known noise: urllib also matches urllib.parse, which never touches the
# network, and socket also matches websocket and socketserver.
socket::socket::raw network access (planner must stay offline)
urllib::urllib::HTTP client (planner must stay offline)
http-client::http\.client::HTTP client (planner must stay offline)

# Optional, NOT enabled. Remove the leading '# ' from a line to ban it too.
# Each active line needs a matching sample file in fixtures/network/ (named
# after the rule) or smoke_test.sh will fail.
# requests::requests\.::third-party HTTP client
# httpx::httpx::third-party HTTP client
# aiohttp::aiohttp::third-party async HTTP client
# ftplib::ftplib::FTP client
# smtplib::smtplib::SMTP client
